\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrix for Mice Protein dataset (k=5). Rows represent true labels, columns represent predicted labels.}}{3}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrix for Isolet dataset (k=5). The diagonal elements represent correct classifications.}}{4}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Classification Error vs. k for Mice Protein dataset. The error rate shows a U-shaped curve, with the minimum occurring at small $k$ values.}}{6}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Classification Error vs. k for Isolet dataset. Performance remains relatively stable across a wide range of $k$ values.}}{6}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces PCA 2D projection for Mice Protein dataset. The first two components (PC1: 19.08\%, PC2: 14.62\%) capture 33.70\% of total variance. Classes show clear separation in the reduced space, with 27 components needed for 95\% variance.}}{9}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces PCA 2D projection for Isolet dataset. The first two components (PC1: 24.11\%, PC2: 8.76\%) capture 32.87\% of total variance. Despite 26 classes, the projection shows reasonable separation, with 177 components needed for 95\% variance.}}{10}{figure.caption.25}%
